After the bug, I found out a simpler way to do the delayed release. Instead of defining a function to reduce the capacity, I simply defined the wayback trajectory to seize the resources, like this:

#Main trajectory of the package
  seize(.,"Shuttle",1)%>%
  seize(.,"Driver_Shuttle",1)%>%
  timeout(function() rexp(1, 1/ShuttleTime))%>% #time spent to come to the warehouse
  release("Driver_Shuttle",1)%>%  #After docking the shuttle the driver is free to take another shuttle

  seize(., "FLT_UL_", 1) %>%
  timeout(function() rexp(1,1/FLT_unloadingTime)) %>% #unloading at the warehouse
  release(.,"FLT_UL_", 1)%>%
  release("Shuttle",1)%>%
  activate(generator = "wayback1")%>%
... the package continues its trajectory

wayback1<-trajectory()%>% #independent wayback trajectory
  seize("Shuttle",1)%>%
  seize("Driver_Shuttle",1)%>%
  timeout(function() rexp(1, 1/WaybackTime1))%>%
  release("Shuttle",1)%>%
  release("Driver_Shuttle",1)

I was having problems with the set_capacity because every time the shuttle was seized its total capacity was reduced, and that limited the resources in use by half of the total. like this:

 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-IMsagI2-JOM/Whffg6Wyc7I/AAAAAAAACnk/I0VZt2AczUgqTkxSgCqU-WTSRQKabxw0ACLcBGAs/s1600/wfunc.png>
And that lead to the bug when the resource was fully in use, the rate of arrivals was increased:

 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-4mzi5SvdJDk/WhffxI9Wk0I/AAAAAAAACno/h_ewRcvDVUIgl6rRRDQNtkFnJqY5n1EMgCLcBGAs/s1600/bug.png>
With the correction, excluding the set_capacity function it looks much better:

 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-WbDIIACAn5g/Whfgar9rChI/AAAAAAAACn0/o8dNhK6AP6Qw8QB3ah8FwI4OyVVPK875gCLcBGAs/s1600/wofunc.png>

But now I have another question regarding the driver. The problem is that after docking the shuttle, the driver is released. The way it is now, after being released, the driver comes back to the pool of resources, which means that he can also be used (seized) to drive the shuttle from the mill to the warehouse. That occurs in the real process, in case there are too many shuttles waiting to go to the warehouse. But that also occurs with a delay. This means that the driver can come the wayback, with or without carrying a shuttle. Do you have any idea on how to simulate this?

I'm sorry that I'm asking so many things, but this is the first time I'm doing Discrete Event Simulation and this is the only place I can discuss.

Thanks,

