I see. My bad: I didn't realise that set_capacity and release must
happen at the same time to avoid this problem. The trajectory I
defined in a previous email should be this instead:

- batch 20
- seize shuttle

- seize driver
- timeout (to warehouses)
- release driver

- set capacity shuttle -1                              <- here instead

- activate generator into other trajectory
- release shuttle
- separate
- ...

Could you test this to see if you obtain results similar to your new
approach? (As discussed, the shuttle must be non-preemptive for this).

> With the correction, excluding the set_capacity function it looks much
> better:

With the new approach you are proposing, note that the shuttle must be
preemptive, and the arrivals you put into the wayback trajectory must
be of a higher priority than the packages.

I think both approaches should give the same results, but I still
think that decreasing the capacity is more accurate with respect to
the real system, because once a shuttle arrives to the warehouse, it
is not a resource that you can use anymore until it finds its way
back.

> But now I have another question regarding the driver. The problem is that
> after docking the shuttle, the driver is released. The way it is now, after
> being released, the driver comes back to the pool of resources, which means
